    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08452v1</id>
    <title>NICE FACT: Diagnosing and Calibrating VLMs in Quantitative Reasoning for Kinematic Physics</title>
    <updated>2026-05-08T20:17:44Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08452v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.08452v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The ability to derive precise spatial and physical insights is a cornerstone of vision-language models (VLMs), yet their poor performances in related spatial intelligence tasks such as physical reasoning remain a fundamental barrier. The community critically lacks a scientific analysis revealing whether VLMs faithfully reach answers or plausibly make guesses. This work aims to provide a fundamental understanding of how VLMs perceive the physical world, and utilize physical laws, while assessing the reliability of model confidence. We propose NICE and FACT, a dual-diagnostic paradigm that explicitly decomposes quantitative reasoning for kinematic physics: FACT diagnoses visual fidelity, physical law comprehension, and temporal grounding. NICE studies our novel neighborhood-informed calibration method and novel metrics to evaluate and calibrate confidence reliability. Evaluated across 6 latest state-of-the-art VLMs, we uncover that models fail to identify visual preconditions or utilize necessary physical laws to reach answers. This work highlights and establishes a standardized diagnostic paradigm to guide the development of faithful, physically-grounded VLMs.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-05-08T20:17:44Z</published>
